   Two suggestions:
(1) To simplify your first compile, I recommend compiling /without/ tiles
first. This will reduce the number of system-specific dependencies, like
that "rpcndr.h" thing MinGW is pulling in. Also, of course, text mode is
The Way NetHack Is Meant To Be Played. ;)

(2) A quick fix for the problem you're seeing right now would be to open
up "Makefile.gcc" in Notepad and add -DSKIP_BOOLEAN to the list of options
in cflags. That is, change

#    Normal
cflags = -c -O -I../include $(DLBFLG) -DUSE_TILES
LFLAGS =
endif

to

#    Normal
cflags = -c -O -I../include $(DLBFLG) -DUSE_TILES -DSKIP_BOOLEAN
LFLAGS =
endif

Then run 'make' again, and see whether your problem goes away.

   FYI, I figured that out by opening up "global.h" in Notepad, searching
for the definition of 'boolean', and noticing that it was surrounded by
#ifndef SKIP_BOOLEAN. Once you get more comfortable with reading the
often-cryptic compiler messages, you should be able to do that on your
own.

